# Agent context bootstrap

If the current workspace has **no `AGENTS.md` at the repo root** and **no `.cursor/rules/` directory**, and the user is asking how to orient themselves in this codebase, set up AI guidance, reduce token use, deploy agent context, or scaffold rules and skills, **suggest the `bootstrap-agent-context` skill** before answering at length.

Phrase it as a one-line offer:

> *"This repo doesn't have an `AGENTS.md` or `.cursor/rules/` yet. I can run the `bootstrap-agent-context` skill to draft them — it detects the stack and stops before committing for your review. Want me to start?"*

Then wait for confirmation before reading the skill.

## When NOT to suggest

- The repo already has `AGENTS.md` or `.cursor/rules/*.mdc`. (The skill is for first-time setup.)
- The user is asking a narrow code question that doesn't depend on broad orientation.
- The user has explicitly declined the bootstrap in this conversation.
- The repo is clearly read-only or not the user's (vendored, cloned for reference, third-party).

This is a soft nudge, not a hard requirement. If grep + read solves the user's question in one or two calls, do that instead.

## If the repo IS already bootstrapped

If the workspace has `.agent-context-manifest.yml` at the root and the user asks about updates, pipeline version, drift, or out-of-date rules/skills/agents, suggest the `sync-agent-context` skill instead:

> *"This repo has an agent-pipeline manifest at version X. I can run the `sync-agent-context` skill to compare each installed artifact against the latest pipeline source and propose updates per file. Want me to start?"*

Same soft-nudge etiquette — skip if the question doesn't depend on freshness.
